A schema has exactly the attributes {P, Q, R, S, T}. To compute a candidate set's closure, repeatedly apply each dependency X -> Y by adding Y whenever X is already present, stopping only when no attribute can be added. A candidate is a minimal key exactly when its closure contains all five attributes and no proper subset has full closure. The active dependencies are R -> Q, Q -> P, P -> S, and Q -> T. Which listed candidate is the unique minimal key?

(A) {P}
(B) {Q}
(C) {R}

Return only JSON: {"answer":"A|B|C","confidence":0-100,"reason":"one concise sentence"}.
